---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EventRecordContext from "../../../components/EventRecordContext.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import { event } from "../../../data/fomc20260729.mjs";
import { ratePath } from "../../../data/fomc20260617.mjs";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-fed-rate-july-2026");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fed-rate-july-2026");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);

// Step-chart geometry from the shared rate path, extended to this meeting.
const W = 720, H = 200, PAD = 8;
const t0 = Date.parse(ratePath.start.date), t1 = Date.parse("2026-07-29");
const vMin = 3.4, vMax = 5.7;
const x = (d) => PAD + ((Date.parse(d) - t0) / (t1 - t0)) * (W - 2 * PAD);
const y = (v) => H - PAD - ((v - vMin) / (vMax - vMin)) * (H - 2 * PAD);
let pts = [`${x(ratePath.start.date)},${y(ratePath.start.value)}`];
let prev = ratePath.start.value;
for (const c of ratePath.changes) {
  pts.push(`${x(c.date)},${y(prev)}`);
  pts.push(`${x(c.date)},${y(c.value)}`);
  prev = c.value;
}
pts.push(`${x("2026-07-29")},${y(prev)}`);
const path = "M" + pts.join(" L");
const endX = x("2026-07-29"), endY = y(prev);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fed-rate/">The federal funds rate</a><span>July 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story">
      <p class="story-kicker"><a href="/events/fed-rate/">The federal funds rate</a> &middot; July 2026 decision</p>
      <h1>{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek">{event.dek}</p>

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-statement">The statement barely moved</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the outlets got right, and what they got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#markets">What the markets said</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value" id={k.live ? "kpi-days" : undefined}>{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label" id={k.live ? "kpi-days-label" : undefined}>{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The Fed held at 3.50&ndash;3.75 percent for the fifth straight meeting, but the vote split 9&ndash;3.</li>
          <li>All three dissenters, Beth Hammack, Neel Kashkari, and Lorie Logan, wanted a quarter-point <em>hike</em>: the first three-dissent split in one direction since September 2016.</li>
          <li>Before the meeting, by the Fed&rsquo;s own account, markets put roughly a one-in-three chance on a hike. The hold won; the argument did not end.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>



      <EventRecordContext subEvents={[{ date: "July 29", label: "Federal Reserve decision", section_anchor: "what-happened" }, { date: "August 28", label: "Warsh at Jackson Hole", section_anchor: "what-next" }]} recordIds={["fomc-statement-2026-07-29", "fomc-minutes-2026-07-29", "fomc-presconf-2026-07-29", "fomc-statement-2026-06-17", "fomc-minutes-2026-06-17", "fomc-projections-2026-06-17", "fed-warsh-2026-08-28", "fed-waller-2026-09-03", "bls-empsit-2026-08"]} />
      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The decision</p>
        <h2>What happened</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The Federal Open Market Committee held the federal funds target range at&nbsp;<strong>3&#8209;1/2 to 3&#8209;3/4 percent</strong> on July 29, by a&nbsp;<strong>9&ndash;3</strong> vote.
          Beth Hammack (Cleveland), Neel Kashkari (Minneapolis), and Lorie Logan (Dallas) voted no; each
          &ldquo;preferred to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point at
          this meeting.&rdquo; <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <details class="receipt"><summary>the record</summary>
          <p>Statement: &ldquo;The Federal Open Market Committee approved the following statement for release by a 9&ndash;3 vote&hellip; Voting against the monetary policy action were Beth M. Hammack, Neel Kashkari, and Lorie K. Logan, who preferred to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point at this meeting.&rdquo;
          Voting for: Warsh, Williams, Barr, Bowman, Cook, Jefferson, Paulson, Powell, Waller (minutes). <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260729a.htm" rel="noreferrer">statement</a> &middot; <a href="/records/outlet-3a654fea6a558369/">minutes</a></p>
        </details>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The minutes, released August 19, give the majority&rsquo;s reason first: <em>&ldquo;most participants
          supported maintaining the current target range,&rdquo;</em> because the information arriving before
          the next meeting <em>&ldquo;could provide more clarity, and correspondingly reduce uncertainty, about
          the inflation outlook.&rdquo;</em> Most expected inflation to <em>&ldquo;step down over the rest of the
          year as the effects of tariffs and earlier energy price increases wane.&rdquo;</em> Nobody at the table
          called inflation acceptable; the disagreement was whether to wait one more data cycle. <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The same minutes suggest the hike case reached beyond the three who voted for it: <em>&ldquo;several
          participants favored an increase of 25 basis points in the target range at this meeting,&rdquo;</em>
          judging that <em>&ldquo;price pressures appeared broad based.&rdquo;</em> The minutes count
          participants, a wider group than the twelve who vote, and do not name or number them, so how much wider
          is not knowable from the record. A few argued a hike now <em>&ldquo;would likely help forestall the need
          for a steeper and potentially more costly sequence of tightening moves at a later stage.&rdquo;</em>
          <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          Asked at the press conference where the disagreement lay, Chair Kevin Warsh said he would <em>&ldquo;let the dissenters speak for themselves,&rdquo;</em> and described <em>&ldquo;overwhelming
          agreement on objectives and authority and commitment&rdquo;</em> with the argument confined to how best
          to get there. His inflation line was categorical: <em>&ldquo;there is no soft implicit target&rdquo;</em>, he
          said, <em>&ldquo;not on this Committee&rsquo;s watch.&rdquo;</em> <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker">History</p>
        <h2>Where this sits</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          The range has been parked at 3.50&ndash;3.75 since <strong>December 11, 2025:</strong> <strong>230 days</strong> as of this meeting, and this was the&nbsp;<strong>fifth consecutive
          meeting</strong> (January, March, April, June, July) to leave it there.&nbsp; <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
        <figure class="rate-chart">
          <svg viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H + 24}`} role="img" aria-label="Fed target range upper bound, 2024 to July 2026: six steps down from 5.50 to 3.75, then flat">
            <path d={path} fill="none" stroke="#7a9cc4" stroke-width="2.5" />
            <circle cx={endX} cy={endY} r="4.5" fill="#c98a1b" />
            <text x={x("2024-02-01")} y={y(5.5) - 8} class="chart-note">5.50 peak</text>
            <text x={x("2024-06-20")} y={y(4.0)} class="chart-note">six cuts, Sep 2024 &rarr; Dec 2025</text>
            <text x={endX - 6} y={endY - 12} text-anchor="end" class="chart-note chart-note-accent">3.75 &middot; held 230 days</text>
            <text x={x("2024-01-05")} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">2024</text>
            <text x={x("2025-01-05")} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">2025</text>
            <text x={x("2026-01-05")} y={H + 16} class="chart-axis">2026</text>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>Upper bound of the target range. Source: Federal Reserve daily history (FRED).</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          Against the full 1982&ndash;2026 daily history of the Fed's target, 16,036 observations, <strong>47 percent</strong> of those days had a lower rate than today's midpoint: the middle of four
          decades of policy. The early years set a single target rate and the later ones a range, so this is a
          rough placement, not a ranking. What is rare is not the level but the argument about it. <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
        <details class="receipt"><summary>computation</summary>
          <p>Combined FRED DFEDTAR (1982&ndash;2008) and DFEDTARU (2008&ndash;) daily series through 2026-08-22:
          16,036 observations; 47 percent sit below the current 3.625 midpoint. Days held: 2025-12-11 to
          2026-07-29 = 230. Meeting count from the Federal Reserve's own 2026 calendar. The full daily series
          is published by the St. Louis Fed (FRED); we work from a copy saved August 23, 2026.</p>
        </details>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          Three dissents at one meeting, all pulling the same way, last happened in&nbsp;<strong>September
          2016</strong>, when Esther George, Loretta Mester, and Eric Rosengren each preferred a hike the
          committee did not deliver. That episode is checked against the record below.&nbsp; <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The split did not appear from nowhere. Six weeks earlier the June vote was 12&ndash;0, but the June
          minutes record that <em>&ldquo;a few participants commented that, in light of these developments,
          there was a case for raising the target range for the federal funds rate, but those participants
          indicated that they supported maintaining the current target range at this meeting.&rdquo;</em> The
          June projections put nine of eighteen year-end rate estimates above the current midpoint, as the <a href="/events/fed-rate/june-2026/">June story</a> showed. July turned a visible minority into three
          recorded votes. <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-statement">
        <p class="sec-kicker">The real signal</p>
        <h2>The statement barely moved</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-computed">
          We compared the July statement to June's, word by word. Outside the vote line, exactly one verb
          changed: June's &ldquo;The Committee <em>reaffirmed</em> its policy of maintaining ample
          reserves&rdquo; became &ldquo;The Committee <em>is continuing</em> its policy.&rdquo; Every other
          sentence, the solid-pace economy, the Middle East conflict, the elevated-inflation line, and the
          closing &ldquo;The Committee will deliver price stability,&rdquo; carried over unchanged. The only
          real addition was the dissent paragraph. <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span>
        </p>
        <details class="receipt"><summary>the comparison</summary>
          <p>Both statements captured from the Federal Reserve's website and diffed sentence by sentence:
          one verb substitution plus the new &ldquo;Voting against&rdquo; paragraph naming Hammack, Kashkari,
          and Logan. <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260617a.htm" rel="noreferrer">June statement</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20260729a.htm" rel="noreferrer">July statement</a></p>
        </details>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          That silence is deliberate. Warsh has pulled the committee back from the forward guidance his
          predecessors used to steer markets, and told reporters the resulting repricing was partly the point: <em>&ldquo;That&rsquo;s part of the reason why we&rsquo;ve been somewhat spare on our words when we
          pulled back from forward guidance.&rdquo;</em> He also called it a transition, not a finished reform: <em>&ldquo;the pullback of forward guidance requires some transition.&rdquo;</em>
          <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          Warsh floated a second change in the room: the minutes record that he <em>&ldquo;observed that six
          scheduled meetings per year, held roughly every two months, would allow more information to accumulate
          between meetings than under current practice,&rdquo;</em> asked for input, and made no decision; <em>&ldquo;any change in practice would not affect the schedule over the balance of 2026.&rdquo;</em>
          The committee now meets eight times a year. <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Cross-source check</p>
        <h2>What the outlets got right, and what they got wrong</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Five outlets' decision coverage, checked against the primary record.</p>

        <div class="outlet-check ok">
          <p class="oc-head"><strong>CBS News</strong>, &ldquo;the fifth consecutive time the central bank has kept interest rates in the target range of 3.5% to 3.75%. The last time Fed officials voted to change interest rates was in December 2025, when it reduced its key rate by 0.25 percentage points.&rdquo;</p>
          <p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> Both halves match: the Fed's daily rate history shows a quarter-point cut on December 11, 2025 and no move since, and the Fed's 2026 calendar shows exactly five meetings from January through July.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="outlet-check ok">
          <p class="oc-head"><strong>AP</strong>, &ldquo;the fifth straight meeting at which the benchmark rate was kept at around 3.6%.&rdquo;</p>
          <p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> The midpoint of the 3.50&ndash;3.75 range is 3.625, and the five-meeting count matches the calendar.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="outlet-check ok">
          <p class="oc-head"><strong>Axios</strong>, &ldquo;the statement was virtually unchanged from the last meeting, offering no clues as to whether or in what circumstances the committee might raise rates.&rdquo;</p>
          <p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">checks out</span> Our own word-by-word comparison found one changed verb plus the dissent paragraph, nothing else.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="outlet-check ok">
          <p class="oc-head"><strong>CNBC</strong>, &ldquo;this is the first time since September 2016 that three policymakers dissented with a unified view of which direction rates should head.&rdquo;</p>
          <p class="oc-verdict"><span class="chip chip-ok">consistent</span> The 2016 anchor checks out against the September 2016 statement: George, Mester, and Rosengren all preferred a hike. NBC and CNN carried the same first-since-2016 line. We verified the anchor; we did not recount every meeting in between.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="outlet-check note">
          <p class="oc-head">Framing worth seeing side by side: the statement's stated reason for holding is the dual mandate; <strong>USA Today</strong> led with President Trump's calls for a cut, <strong>CBS</strong> with the Iran war's effect on energy costs, and <strong>Axios</strong> with the internal dissent. All are in the record; the emphasis is the editorial choice.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Verified</p>
        <h2>Claim checked against the record</h2>
        <div class="checked-block">
          <p class="cb-label">The recurring premise: inflation above target &ldquo;for more than five years&rdquo;</p>
          <p>The June story confirmed this claim through May 2026. It is still true, and one month longer: PCE inflation has now printed above 2&nbsp;percent year-over-year for&nbsp;<strong>64 consecutive months</strong> since March 2021. At the time of this meeting the latest reading was May's 4.1&nbsp;percent; June data, released after the meeting, came in at 3.7&nbsp;percent, the easing the doves pointed to. <span class="chip chip-computed">computed</span></p>
          <details class="receipt"><summary>computation</summary>
            <p>FRED PCEPI monthly index, year-over-year change; every month March 2021 through June 2026
            exceeds 2.0 percent; May 2026 = 4.08 percent, June 2026 = 3.67 percent. The minutes' own staff
            review states total PCE inflation &ldquo;was 4.1 percent in May.&rdquo; The series is published by
            the St. Louis Fed (FRED); we work from a copy saved August 23, 2026.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker">On the ground</p>
        <h2>Who feels it</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-official">
          While the committee argued over a quarter point, the rates households face held high: the 30-year
          fixed mortgage averaged&nbsp;<strong>6.66&nbsp;percent</strong> the week of the decision.
          Unemployment stood at&nbsp;<strong>4.2&nbsp;percent</strong> in the June reading available at the
          meeting; July printed 4.1 afterward. <span class="chip chip-official">official data</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="what-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Aftermath</p>
        <h2>What happened next</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          The minutes arrived&nbsp;<strong>August 19</strong> and confirmed the hike camp extends beyond the
          three dissenters: several participants favored an increase, and <em>&ldquo;many participants
          assessed that policy tightening would likely be necessary if inflation did not decline.&rdquo;</em>
          <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>August 28.</strong> At Jackson Hole, Warsh described the July hold as a majority choosing to
          wait: <em>&ldquo;A good majority of my colleagues and I thought the wiser course was to await new
          information in the intermeeting period.&rdquo;</em> He said he <em>&ldquo;would be hard pressed to
          describe broad financial conditions as restrictive,&rdquo;</em> put <em>&ldquo;the responsibility for
          65 months of sustained, elevated inflation&rdquo;</em> on the central bank, and declined to say what
          September holds: <em>&ldquo;I stand here today committed to a discipline, not to a
          decision.&rdquo;</em> <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          <strong>September 3.</strong> Governor Christopher Waller, who voted to hold in July, set out both
          doors: <em>&ldquo;If there is continued progress toward our 2 percent goal, then I am willing to
          support holding the policy rate at its current level. But if inflation comes in hot, I would consider
          a rate hike.&rdquo;</em> His test is the August inflation data due before the meeting. <span class="chip chip-record">their words</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-official">
          <strong>September 4.</strong> The August jobs report gave the hawks nothing to point at and the doves
          nothing to fear: payrolls up 162,000, unemployment unchanged at 4.1 percent, and June and July revised
          up by 11,000 and 44,000. The story of that report is on the <a href="/events/jobs/august-2026/">August jobs page</a>. <span class="chip chip-official">official data</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-note">
          Watching: the August consumer price index on September 11, the test Waller named; then the September
          15&ndash;16 meeting, which brings refreshed projections and shows whether three dissents become a
          majority. Added September 8, 2026.
        </p>
      </section>
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />

      <section id="markets">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Pricing</p>
        <h2>What the markets said</h2>
        <p class="railed rail-record">
          For once the pricing story is in the Fed's own record. The minutes' desk briefing: while no action
          was the base case, <em>&ldquo;the market priced in about a one-in-three chance of an increase in
          the target range,&rdquo;</em> and at longer horizons <em>&ldquo;the market was fully pricing in a
          25 basis point hike by the September meeting.&rdquo;</em> <span class="chip chip-record">record</span>
        </p>
        <p class="railed rail-outlet">
          After the hold, NBC News quoted CME's FedWatch tool putting the odds of a September quarter-point
          hike at&nbsp;<strong>59 percent</strong>; CBS News, in the same hour, put the same odds at <em>&ldquo;roughly 53%.&rdquo;</em> Both read the same futures market, so the gap is timing, not
          disagreement, and neither is a second source for the other. <span class="chip chip-outlet">reported pricing</span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
        <p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p>
        <h2>The records</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, by kind.</p>

        <h3 class="src-group">Our records</h3>
        <ul class="record-links">
          <li><a href="/claims/fomc-statement-july-29-2026-three-dissents-for-hike/">
            <span class="rl-title">FOMC statement: decision day</span>
            <span class="rl-meta">Released 2:00 p.m. July 29 &middot; the 9&ndash;3 vote and the three dissents, in the Committee's words</span></a></li>
          <li><a href="/claims/fomc-minutes-july-29-2026-three-dissents-for-hike/">
            <span class="rl-title">FOMC minutes: the discussion behind it</span>
            <span class="rl-meta">Released August 19 &middot; the hike case, wider than the vote</span></a></li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="/records/fomc-presconf-2026-07-29/">Chairman Warsh&rsquo;s press conference, July 29</a> &middot; the transcript, for his words on the dissents and forward guidance</li>
          <li><a href="/records/fomc-statement-2026-06-17/">FOMC statement, June 17</a>, <a href="/records/fomc-minutes-2026-06-17/">June minutes</a>, and <a href="/records/fomc-projections-2026-06-17/">June projections</a> &middot; the statement comparison and the split that preceded the vote</li>
          <li><a href="/records/fed-warsh-2026-08-28/">Warsh at Jackson Hole, August 28</a> and <a href="/records/fed-waller-2026-09-03/">Waller on the outlook, September 3</a> &middot; what happened next</li>
          <li><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-08/">The August jobs report, September 4</a> &middot; what happened next</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20160921a.htm" rel="noreferrer">FOMC statement, September 21, 2016</a> &middot; the last three-dissent split in one direction, used to check the outlets' claim</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm" rel="noreferrer">The Fed's meeting calendar</a> &middot; the five-meeting count and the September 15&ndash;16 date</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="src-group">Official data</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DFEDTARU" rel="noreferrer">Fed target range, daily, 1982&ndash;2026</a> &middot; the chart, the 230-day count, the 47th percentile</li>
          <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/PCEPI" rel="noreferrer">PCE price index</a> &middot; the 64-month above-target streak</li>
          <li><a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/MORTGAGE30US" rel="noreferrer">30-year mortgage average</a> and <a href="https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/UNRATE" rel="noreferrer">unemployment rate</a> &middot; who feels it</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="src-group">Coverage checked</h3>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="/records/outlet-7f3aa3f02503cd13/">CNBC</a>, <a href="/records/outlet-0329441c4201b94b/">AP</a>, and <a href="/records/outlet-81580f2e91b3dfa8/">CBS News</a>, decision day</li>
          <li><a href="/records/outlet-eb2cef3bd525271c/">Axios</a> &middot; direct capture was blocked by the site, so its text was recovered through an assisted web reader and kept with this page's sources</li>
          <li><a href="/records/outlet-1dccf05cb4b25801/">NBC News</a> and CBS News &middot; carry the two post-decision odds figures quoted above</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="sources-line"><a href="/methodology">How we check</a> &middot; every figure above traces to one of these sources; captured copies are kept so the record stays verifiable if pages change.</p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
